                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I was initially drawn to The Surge by its aesthetic; given the genre, it looked like a highly appetizing prospect—moving away from gothic, medieval, and fantasy tropes in favor of a sci-fi adventure. And I have to say, this offering—already appealing for its looks—turned out to be a Michelin-star experience in every other regard... or well, almost every one.\n\nTo start at the beginning: it is visually stunning across the board. The designs of the locations, enemies, bosses, weapons, and armor are so full of charisma, personality, and distinctiveness that it’s almost overwhelming. While I love some designs more than others, the entire art department is a 10/10. I especially appreciate the effort made to ensure no piece of equipment is useless; while this might happen occasionally with weapons, every armor set is highly distinct, offering unique playstyles and benefits that go far beyond mere aesthetics or higher stats.\n\nRegarding gameplay, I must admit my lack of experience; I’m not deeply versed in the genre—having only played Mortal Shell and Stonemachia previously—so my perspective is somewhat limited. Even so, it felt quite distinct from the genre standard—both from my own experience and what I’ve seen elsewhere. It features primarily strategic combat that factors in various variables, allowing for adaptation to all kinds of approaches and playstyles. These vary based not just on your strategy, but also on your armor, weaponry, and modifiers. You attack specific body parts of your opponent, which opens the door to mechanics beyond simple damage and resistance—incorporating executions and resource gathering, as you receive different rewards depending on which enemy limbs you sever. There are quite a few variables to consider if you want to achieve the best results. Beyond that, I’d say the gameplay is simply extremely satisfying—one of the best experiences I’ve had, not just in the genre but overall. The impact of the hits, the weight of your character, and the sheer violence of your attacks and executions create a deeply satisfying loop.\n\nIf I had to complain about anything, it would be the difficulty and the story—though neither is a major issue. The game has some pretty noticeable difficulty spikes in certain sections, and at times, some encounters can feel quite unfair; this is especially apparent when moving between areas. On a slightly more serious note, I’d also point to the fights against the third and fourth bosses; for me, those experiences felt both boring and unfair. That was a shame, given the high quality of the other three bosses in the base game and the various ones found in the DLCs.\n\nAs for the story, it isn’t bad at all; it has good moments, a solid plot, a fascinating backstory, and a serviceable conclusion. Yet, I simply didn't care about it—though it didn't quite feel like a secondary element, either. It was packed with endless dialogue and audio logs, and one of the DLCs—A Walk in the Park—tried to add depth to a protagonist who just isn't interesting; at times, I even struggle to remember his name.\n\nBut as I said, these aren't major problems—just minor annoyances. They don't stop me from saying that The Surge is an incredible experience that you should definitely try.",
